From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Builder <builder@example.invalid>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] usb: phy_meson_usb: pulse the controller reset per phy, not
 per module

meson_u2phy_usb_reset() guards the controller reset pulse with a
function-static counter:

	static int	init_count;
	...
	if (!init_count) {
		init_count++;
		writel((0x1 << phy->usb_reset_bit), phy->reset_regs);
	}

Static to the function, so the pulse fires once per module load rather
than once per phy.

This board binds two phy instances with different bits. meson-s7d.dtsi
gives usbphy0's u2p@0 usb-reset-bit = <5> and usbphy1's u2p@0
usb-reset-bit = <4>, and the board DTS sets both nodes okay. Each device
calls meson_u2phy_aml_init() independently, which calls this function.
The first pulses bit 5; the second finds init_count non-zero and returns
having written nothing. Bit 4 is never pulsed.

Why the same text is correct in 5.15. The 5.15 driver that runs this
hardware, drivers/usb/crgdrdphy/phy-aml-crg-drd-usb2.c, carries the
identical function with the identical static counter -- it was
transcribed unchanged -- but 5.15 models the whole block as one device
with a port count and iterates inside it,
"for (i = 0; i < phy->portnum; i++)". With a single instance, "once per
module load" and "once per device" are the same statement, and the
static is a correct way to say do this once. 6.12 splits the block into
two devices bound separately; portnum is gone and each node hardcodes
one port. The guard's meaning changed underneath text that did not.

So this restores the original intent rather than deleting the guard. The
counter becomes per-instance state on struct amlogic_usb_v2, which is
what meson_aml_u2phy_parse() kzalloc()s per device, so it starts zero
without an initialiser. A phy still gets exactly one pulse; there are
now two phys getting one each instead of one phy getting one and one
getting none.

What this is not. It is not the cause of the phy_rdy failure recorded in
docs/task/BUG-010.md, and it was disqualified there on the symmetry of
that failure rather than left untested: usbphy0 does receive its pulse
on bit 5 and failed identically to usbphy1 -- same "wait for ready
timeout", same "set pll failed", same "phy init failed --> -110". A
cause acting on exactly one of two phys cannot produce a symmetric
failure on both. That failure was the SCMI clock reference and is fixed
and merged. USB works today with this defect present.

So no change in behaviour is expected from this patch on this board.
It is fixed because the code is wrong and produces no error while being
wrong -- any later path that depends on usbphy1 having been reset, a
suspend/resume cycle, a module reload, a second controller coming up,
inherits it silently.

Scope, stated because "once per phy" and "once per bit" are not the same
statement on every SoC in this tree. Several dtsi files give two or three
u2p nodes the *same* usb-reset-bit -- meson-a4 and mesont7c at 4,
meson-s4, meson-s4d and meson-sc2 at 4, mesonsm1 at 2,
mesont5m_reva at 3. There the static guard was pulsing a shared bit once
and this patch pulses it once per node, so the second write re-pulses a
controller reset that the first node has already brought up. sm1 is
unaffected in practice, since bit 2 takes the read-modify-write arm
above and a repeated OR is idempotent; the bit-4 SoCs would see a real
second pulse.

None of them is this board. s7d and s7 are the two that give their two
nodes distinct bits, 5 and 4, which is the case the static guard breaks
and the case this build is for. A guard keyed on (reset_regs, bit)
rather than on the instance would be right everywhere, but it would put
module-global state back to do it, and this is a board build. Recorded
so the difference is not discovered by someone porting this forward.

Whether bit 4 is already released at handover is not established here.
If U-Boot leaves it de-asserted the missing pulse has no observable
effect on any current path, which is consistent with USB working.
Nothing in this patch measures the reset register; 0016 adds the print
that shows which bits the driver pulses.
---
--- a/common_drivers/include/linux/amlogic/usb-v2-common.h
+++ b/common_drivers/include/linux/amlogic/usb-v2-common.h
@@ -140,6 +140,13 @@
 	u32 phy_reg_reset_level_bit[USB_PHY_MAX_NUMBER];
 	u32 phy_reg_reset_bit;
 	u32 usb_reset_bit;
+	/* Set once meson_u2phy_usb_reset() has pulsed this phy's
+	 * controller reset bit. Per instance, not per module: 6.12
+	 * binds one device per phy node, so a function-static guard
+	 * fires once per module load and leaves every phy after the
+	 * first one unpulsed.
+	 */
+	bool usb_reset_done;
 	u32 usb_comb_reset_bit;
 	u32 phy_apb_reset_bit;
 	u32 pm_controller:1;
--- a/common_drivers/drivers/usb/phy_meson_usb/phy-meson-aml-usb2-com.c
+++ b/common_drivers/drivers/usb/phy_meson_usb/phy-meson-aml-usb2-com.c
@@ -29,8 +29,6 @@
 /* Reset usb controller. */
 int meson_u2phy_usb_reset(struct amlogic_usb_v2 *phy)
 {
-	static int	init_count;
-
 	if (phy->usb_reset_bit == -1U)
 		return 0;
 
@@ -41,8 +39,8 @@
 
 	dev_dbg(phy->dev, "%s initial: 0x%x.\n", __func__,
 				readl(phy->reset_regs + phy->reset_level));
-	if (!init_count) {
-		init_count++;
+	if (!phy->usb_reset_done) {
+		phy->usb_reset_done = true;
 		if (phy->usb_reset_bit == 2)
 			writel((readl(phy->reset_regs) |
 				(0x1 << phy->usb_reset_bit)), phy->reset_regs);
-- 
